# Deciding temporal specifications: sat, unsat, valid and normalize over
# always/sometimes formulas.
#
# See demos/README.md for how to run this script.

# demo_1.4 decided formulas about a single, fixed point in time. The same
# commands decide whole temporal specifications -- formulas built with always
# and sometimes over streams -- and that is the central claim of the Tau
# language: whether a specification can be executed indefinitely, whatever its
# inputs, is a question with a computable answer.

# A specification is satisfiable when, for every input, at each step there
# exist outputs matching it that do not depend on future inputs. The echo
# specification is the simplest satisfiable example:

sat always o1[t] = i1[t]

# Satisfiability is decided against all possible input sequences. The next
# specification demands an output disjoint from the input; since the input may
# be 1, the output must then be 0, which the second conjunct forbids. No
# program can promise this, so the specification is unsatisfiable:

sat always (o1[t] & i1[t] = 0 && o1[t] != 0)

# The interplay of always and sometimes is decided too. Repeating the previous
# output forever makes the output constant, while the sometimes part demands
# that it differs from the value two steps earlier -- a contradiction:

sat (always o1[t] = o1[t-1]) && (sometimes o1[t] != o1[t-2])

# unsat answers the complementary question, so it reports T where sat reports
# F. A constant-0 output can never sometimes be 1:

unsat (always o1[t] = 0) && (sometimes o1[t] = 1)

# valid asks whether every program satisfies the specification:

valid always (o1[t] = 0 || o1[t] != 0)

# normalize works on temporal specifications as well, minimizing each temporal
# part rather than answering T or F -- unless the specification is decided
# outright. Note how the sometimes part comes back rewritten as an equation:

n (always i1[t]o1[t] = 0) && (sometimes o1[t] = 1)

# A specification with a lookback is satisfiable even though nothing pins its
# first step: constraints mentioning t-1 only bind from step 1 onwards.

sat always o1[t] = i1[t-1]

# Constant time constraints (demo_1.1) participate in the decision. The
# specification below writes 0 for the first three steps and 1 afterwards;
# deciding it makes the engine unroll the constrained steps, and the printed
# fixpoint shows that unrolling, including internal flag streams named _f*:

sat always ([t < 3] -> o1[t] = 0) && ([t >= 3] -> o1[t] = 1)

# Tightening the constraint into a contradiction flips the answer -- at step 0
# both guards apply, and the output cannot be 0 and 1 at once:

sat always ([t < 3] -> o1[t] = 0) && ([t >= 0] -> o1[t] = 1)

# Deciding a specification is exactly what the run command of demo_3.1 does
# before executing it: run refuses a specification whose sat check fails,
# because no program could execute it indefinitely.

quit
